  "full_response": "Certainly! Below are sample connection strings for each of the specified databases, following your format.\n\n### MongoDB\n```plaintext\nmongodb://user:pass@host:27017\n```\n- `user:pass` - Username and password for authentication.\n- `host` - The address of the MongoDB server (e.g., `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, or fully qualified domain name).\n- `27017` - The default port for MongoDB.\n\n### PostgreSQL\n```plaintext\npostgresql://user:pass@host:5432/dbname\n```\n- `user:pass` - Username and password for authentication.\n- `host` - The address of the PostgreSQL server.\n- `5432` - The default port for PostgreSQL.\n- `dbname` - The name of the database to connect to.\n\n### Redis\n```plaintext\nredis://:password@host:6379\n```\n- `:` - Place a colon if there is no user (defaults to `redis`).\n- `password` - The password for authentication.\n- `host` - The address of the Redis server.\n- `6379` - The default port for Redis.\n\n### MSSQL (SQL Server)\n```plaintext\nServer=host;Database=dbname;User Id=user;Password=pass;\n```\n- `Server=host` - The address of the SQL Server.\n- `Database=dbname` - The name of the database to connect to.\n- `User Id=user` - The username for authentication.\n- `Password=pass` - The password for authentication.\n\nEach connection string can be modified further based on additional requirements such as database selection, SSL usage, and more, depending on the specific application or context.",
